=== Theorem 9.1

The group $U_n$ is cyclic iff $n=1,2,4,p^e," or "p^(2e)$, where $p$ is an odd prime.

==== Pf.

===== $(arrow.l.double)$

We only left with the case $n=2p^e$.\
By the multiplicativity, $phi(2p^e)=phi(2)phi(p^e)=phi(p^e)$.
Let $g$ be a primitive root $mod p^e$, then so is $g+p^e$.
One of $g$ and $g+p^e$ must be odd.
So we have an odd primitive root $h mod p^e$.
We claim $h$ is a prim. root $mod 2p^e$.
By its construction, $h$ is coprime to 2 and p.
So $h$ is a unit $mod p^e$, we have that $phi(p^e)|i$.
But $phi(2p^e)=phi(p^e)$, so $phi(2p^e)|i$.
Therefore, $h$ has order $phi(2p^e)$ in $U_(2p^e)$, and thus a prim. root.

===== $(=>)$

If $n!=1,2,3,p^e" or "2p^e$, then we must in one of the followity:
+ $n=2^e (e>=3)$
+ $n=2^e dot p^f (e>=2,f>=1" and "p" odd prime")$
+ $n$ is divisible by at least two odd primes.

In case (1), $U_n$ is now cyclic by Prof. 8.13.
Case (2) and (3) are covered Lemma 8.17.

Recall Corollary 7.8 and now the structure of $U_(p^e)$ is known.

