== 9.1

One motivation. Solve quadratic equastions in $ZZ_n$.
Assume that $gcd(2a, n)=1$.
The quadratic congruence $a x^2+b x+c equiv 0 (mod n)$ has a solution iff $Delta = b^2 - 4 a c$ admits a square root $s$ in $ZZ_n$.
In this case, the original form $x=(-b plus.minus sqrt(Delta))/(2a)$ can be interperted as $(-b plus.minus s)(2a)^(-1)$.

=== Def 9.8

An element $a in U_n$ is a quadratic residue $mod n$ if $a=s^2$ for some $s in U_n$; the set of such quad. res. is denoted by $Q_n$.

==== Rmk:

If $S$ exists in $ZZ_n$, then $S$ must be in $U_n$ because
$ 1 equiv a dot a^(-1) = s^2 dot a^(-1) = s dot (s dot a^(-1)) $

=== Ex. 9.9

$Q_11={1,4,9,5,3}, Q_12={1}$

The number of the square root of $a in Q_n$ does not depend on $a$.
Suppose that $s^2=a$ for some $s in U_n$, then we can write any $t in U_n$ as $t=s dot x$ for some unique $x in U_n$.
Then $t^2=a$ is equivalent to $x^2=1 mod n$.
The number $N$ of solutions for $x^2=1$ is given by Example 5.3.\
So $abs(Q_n) dot N = |U_n| => |Q_n|=phi(n)/N$.

=== Lemma 9.10

$Q_n$ is a subgroup of $U_n$.
Moreover, the map $theta: U_n -> Q_n$ given by $theta(s)=s^2$ is an eprodmorphism (=homomorphism + surjection).

==== Pf.

Check that $Q_n$ contains $1$ and closed under product, inverse.
Checking the map is a homomorphism.(all easy!)

=== Rmk 9.11

The kernel $"Ker"theta$ of $theta$ is exactly the set of solutions to $x^2 equiv 1 mod n$.
We have an isomorphism $U_n\/"Ker"theta tilde.rev.equiv Q_n$, which also explains $abs(Q_n)=phi(n)/N$.

=== Lemma 9.12

Let $n=n_1 n_2 dots n_k$ where the integer $n_i$ are mutually coprime.
Then $a in Q_n$ iff $a in Q_(n_i)$ for each $i$.

==== Pf.

The decomposition $U_n tilde.rev.equiv U_(n_1) times U_(n_1) times dots times U_(n_k)$ restricts to the subgroup $Q_n$.\
This is because an element in $U_n$ is a square iff each of its components in $U_n$ is a square.

=== Lemma 9.13

Suppose that there is a primitive root $g mod n$ for $n > 2$.
The $Q_n$ is also cyclic group of $phi(n)/2$ generated by $g^2$.

==== Pf.

The condition $n>2$ ensure that $phi(n)$ is even.
We claim that $Q_n$ precisely consists of elements of the form $g^(2i)$.
Clearly $g^(2i) in Q_n$.
Conversely, if $a in Q_n$, then $a=(g^j)^2$.
Hence, $Q_n$ is generated by $g^2$ (and has order $phi(n)/2$).

=== Lemma 9.14

Let $p$ be an odd prime.
Then $a in Q_(p^e)$ iff $a in Q_p$.

==== Pf.

We know from Theorem 9.1 that there is a primitive root $g mod p^e$.
So $Q_(p^e)$ consists of even powers of $g$.
But $g$ is also a prim. root $mod p$.
So $Q_p$ also consists of even powers of $g$.

=== Lemma 9.15

Let $a$ be an odd integer. Then
+ $a in Q_2$
+ $a in Q_4 "iff" a equiv 1 mod 4$
+ $a in Q_(2^e)(e>=3) "iff" a equiv 1 mod 8$

==== Pf.

(1) and (2) are easy to be verified.
For (3) we may write $a=plus.minus 5^i$ according Prop. 8.13.
So quad. res. are of the form $5^(2i)$.
Since $5^2 equiv 1 mod 8, 5^(2i) equiv 1 mod 8$.

Both $5^i$ and the elements $a equiv 1 mod 8$ account for exactly one quarter of $U_(2^e)$.
The two sets must be equal.

